Hand file operations to the system's file operations window.

Copies, moves, links, moves to the Trash and deletions started from the
desktop ran with libfm's own progress dialog: a bar and a line of text,
unlike the operations window every other file operation on the system
shows (with its speed graph, pause, and the same questions for names that
are taken), and outside that window's queue.

fm_file_ops_job_run_with_progress() now hands such a job over to LikeOS
Explorer's operations window when every file is on a local disk:
likeos-explorer --copy-to=FOLDER, --move-to=FOLDER, --link-to=FOLDER,
--trash or --delete with the files, and --empty-trash for the Trash
itself.  The command passes them to the running Explorer over the session
bus (or runs them itself, opening no window but the operations window, and
ends when they are done).  The confirmations libfm asks before moving to
the Trash, deleting and emptying the Trash are asked as before; Explorer
asks nothing more.

Everything else -- attribute changes, restoring from the Trash, files not
on a local disk -- runs in libfm with its dialog as before, and so does
every job when likeos-explorer is not installed or cannot be started.

